   {
    "id": "events-idea",
    "eyebrow": "The idea",
    "heading": "A few blocks of 1s, and five things that can happen to them",
    "html": [
     "<div class=\"col\"><p>After its first few steps this machine keeps its tape very tidy: a handful of solid blocks of 1s, with one to four 0s between them. Some blocks are enormous, and the machine spends almost all of its time sweeping across them. But at certain moments the head comes to rest in the same position: in state C, on a single 0, with a block of 1s on each side. Call such a moment an <b>anchor</b>.</p></div>",
     "<div style=\"display:flex;align-items:flex-end;gap:0;margin:26px 0 6px;font:12px var(--mono)\"><div style=\"flex:3 1 0;height:26px;background:var(--muted);border-radius:3px;color:var(--bg);text-align:center;line-height:26px;overflow:hidden;white-space:nowrap\">older blocks</div><div style=\"flex:0 0 42px;text-align:center;color:var(--muted)\">0 0 0</div><div style=\"flex:4 1 0;height:26px;background:var(--accent);border-radius:3px;color:var(--bg);text-align:center;line-height:26px;overflow:hidden;white-space:nowrap\">m ones</div><div style=\"flex:0 0 3px;height:34px;background:var(--head);position:relative\"><span style=\"position:absolute;top:-18px;left:-4px;color:var(--head);font-weight:600\">C</span></div><div style=\"flex:0 0 14px;text-align:center;color:var(--muted)\">0</div><div style=\"flex:5 1 0;height:26px;background:var(--kernel);border-radius:3px;color:var(--bg);text-align:center;line-height:26px;overflow:hidden;white-space:nowrap\">c ones</div><div style=\"flex:0 0 28px;text-align:center;color:var(--muted)\">0 0</div><div style=\"flex:2 1 0;height:26px;background:var(--muted);border-radius:3px;color:var(--bg);text-align:center;line-height:26px;overflow:hidden;white-space:nowrap\">more</div></div>",
     "<div class=\"col\"><p>Write m for the number of 1s just left of the head (blue) and c for the number just right of it (green). What the machine does between one anchor and the next depends only on m, on how many 0s lie to the left of that block, and on whether anything lies further left. There are five cases, and each one is simple arithmetic on the block lengths:</p></div>",
     "<table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.95em\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:6px 10px 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-strong)\">event</th><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:6px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-strong)\">when</th><th style=\"text-align:left;padding:6px 0 6px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-strong)\">what happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-family:var(--mono)\">split</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">m is even, at least 4, and has three or more 0s on its left</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 0 8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">A quarter of the block, &lfloor;m/4&rfloor;, goes to the far side of the gap. Three quarters go to the right block. The remainder, 0 or 2, stays.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-family:var(--mono)\">odd</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">m is odd, at least 3, and there is more tape further left</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 0 8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">The block loses 2, the 0 on its left turns into a 1 (so the gap shrinks by one, and the block joins its neighbour when the gap closes), and the right block gains 3.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-family:var(--mono)\">fold</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">m is odd, at least 3, and nothing is further left</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 0 8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">The odd round repeats by itself until one 1 is left: every 2 ones become 3 on the right. The right block gains 3(m&minus;1)/2. <b>This is the only place where the tape grows by a fixed fraction.</b></td></tr><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-family:var(--mono)\">shift</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">m is even and has one or two 0s on its left</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 0 8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">The block is too close to its neighbour to be split. The head steps over it, and the block (minus one) is now on the right.</td></tr><tr><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px 8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-family:var(--mono)\">zero, one, two</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">m is 0, 1 or 2: what a split leaves behind</td><td style=\"vertical-align:top;padding:8px 0 8px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)\">The few remaining 1s are handed across the gap. In most of these cases the head then runs to the right behind a marker <code>0 1</code>, the <em>carrier</em>, which passes whole blocks and turns into three 1s at the first pair of 0s or at the end of the tape. A new block of 3 starts there.</td></tr></tbody></table>",
     "<div class=\"col\"><p>None of this is a guess. Each rule is a theorem checked by Lean for every block length and every surrounding tape, and together they give an exact, fast model of the machine: instead of simulating billions of single steps, apply one rule per anchor. The next two sections let you try that.</p></div>"
    ]
   },
