   {
    "id": "events-cycle",
    "eyebrow": "Emergent behaviour",
    "heading": "Cut into quarters, glue back together, grow by half",
    "html": [
     "<div class=\"col\"><p>Seen from far away, the run repeats one pattern at ever larger sizes.</p><ol><li><b>One big block.</b> After a fold the tape is a single 1, the head, and one block: <code>1 C&gt; 0 1<sup>N</sup></code>.</li><li><b>Cutting.</b> The block moves to the left of the head and is split: a quarter stays on the left, three quarters go right. The three quarters come back to the head and are split again, and again, leaving a row of pieces, each about three quarters of the one before. The cutting stops when a remainder or a parity does not fit.</li><li><b>Gluing.</b> Odd rounds close the gaps between the pieces, and shifts carry the head back to the left.</li><li><b>Growing.</b> When an odd block reaches the left end of the tape it is folded: every 2 ones become 3.</li></ol><p>At event 27 the tape is <code>1 C&gt; 0 1<sup>36</sup></code>. Four events later it is <code>1<sup>34</sup> C&gt; 0 1<sup>9</sup></code>, and the split cuts 34 into 8 (left), 24 (right, making 33) and a remainder of 2. The pieces are glued together again by odd rounds, 36 is split into 9 and 27, and after more splits, shifts and three folds (event 65) the tape is <code>1 C&gt; 0 1<sup>102</sup></code>.</p><p>The sizes N of these single blocks are 36, 102, 192, 549, 1113, 5592, 60876, 106005, 343155, 591204, &hellip; Each is larger than the last by a factor that is often close to 3/2, but not always, because how many folds and odd rounds happen in between depends on the remainders of the block lengths modulo 4 and on their parities. That is what makes the machine <em>Collatz-like</em>: its future is decided by the low digits of numbers that keep being multiplied by 3/4 and 3/2, and nobody knows how to predict those.</p><p>The sister machine <a href=\"../1RB1LD_1LC0RC_0RF1RA_0LE1LA_1RC0LA_---0RB/explainer.html\"><code>1RB1LD_1LC0RC_0RF1RA_0LE1LA_1RC0LA_---0RB</code></a> does the same thing with a gap of two 0s for a split instead of three.</p></div>"
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "halting",
    "eyebrow": "Halting",
    "heading": "What a halt would need",
    "html": [
     "<div class=\"col\"><p>The machine stops only if it reads a 0 in state F. The rules above cover every situation the run has met so far, and none of them halts. But they do not cover every tape one could write down. No rule is given when</p><ul><li>the right block is too short for a small case (fewer than 3 ones for <code>zero</code>, fewer than 2 for <code>one</code>),</li><li>the carrier meets a single 1 standing alone after a single 0, or lands directly on a 1.</li></ul><p>Small tapes of these kinds often do halt. For example <code>1<sup>5</sup> 0 0 <b>C&gt;</b> 0</code> (nothing on the right at all) halts after 5 steps, and <code>1<sup>5</sup> 0 0 <b>C&gt;</b> 0 1<sup>3</sup> 0 1</code> (the carrier meets a lone 1) halts after 11 steps. So the machine is not a provable non-halter by its rules alone: it halts if and only if the run ever reaches one of these uncovered tapes and the plain machine then stops.</p><p>In the run from blank tape that has not happened in the first 2,999,995 events. Whether it ever does is the open question.</p></div>"
    ]
   },
   {
    "id": "proved",
    "eyebrow": "Lean",
    "heading": "What is proved, and what is not",
    "html": [
     "<div class=\"col\"><ul><li><b>Every rule</b>, for every block length and every surrounding tape (Lean kernel): <code>c_split</code>, <code>c_odd</code>, <code>c_fold</code>, <code>c_shift</code>, <code>c_zero</code>, <code>c_one</code>, <code>c_two</code>, <code>c_oneE</code>, <code>c_oneO</code>, <code>c_pass</code> and the carrier&rsquo;s arrival <code>arr_sem</code>, in <code>lean/Events.lean</code>, from single rounds in <code>lean/EventRules.lean</code>.</li><li><b>The rules are the machine</b> (kernel): <code>F_sem</code> says that the map F (a rule at an anchor it covers, one plain machine step anywhere else) follows the real machine exactly, and <code>halts_iff</code> says the machine halts from blank tape if and only if repeating F from the blank tape reaches a halting configuration.</li><li><b>If the rules never run out, the machine never halts</b> (kernel): <code>events_forever</code>.</li><li><b>No halt in the first 10<sup>137</sup> steps</b> (kernel, 29,996 events evaluated inside Lean): <code>no_halt_kernel</code> in <code>lean/Far.lean</code>.</li><li><b>No halt in the first 10<sup>13275</sup> steps</b> (compiled evaluation, 2,999,995 events): <code>no_halt_native</code>.</li><li><b>This page&rsquo;s JavaScript</b> is a transcription of the Lean definitions. <code>explainer/test.js</code> checks it against the plain machine: every example of the rule viewer, and the first 467 events of the run (4&middot;10<sup>8</sup> machine steps).</li></ul><p><b>Not proved:</b> that the rules never run out. Nothing here decides whether the machine halts.</p></div>"
    ]
